  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bipartite social dialogue mechanism strengthened between employers&apos; organization (Employers Confederation of Thailand) and four workers organizations (State Enterprises Workers&apos; Relations Confederation (SERC), Labour Congress of Thai Labour (LCT), Thai Trade Union Congress (TTUC) and National Congress Private Industrial of Employees (NCPE))&#x2013; helped build shared understanding of the challenges posed by COVID to businesses and workers and develop a bipartite consensus on way forward. Constituents formed the national working group to have bipartite SD on a regular basis.

Bipartite dialogue led to consensus on workplace consultation and cooperation arrangements for productivity and dealing with grievances and disputes at enterprise level. Policy and procedural guidelines were adopted aimed at facilitating the development of enterprise-level grievance procedures.
 ILO supported the social dialogue process between workers and Employers organizations &#x2013; through online dialogues and capacity building on workplace consultations and cooperation arrangements and grievance handling. ILO developed draft policy and procedures in line with the principles recommended in ILO&apos;s IRToolkit and it was adapted by constituents after discussions and negotiations. 

Note: this work has been jointly delivered by INWORK, ACTEMP and ACTRAV Specialists.</narrative>
   </description>
